    "street_relationship": {
      "group": "site",
      "binding": "specified",
      "status": "drafted",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rule": "A near-blind street wall is pierced only by small, high, grilled (reja) openings and the single zaguan door. Unlike the Moorish palace tradition it descends from, the passage is straight, not bent, and it ends at a cancela -- a light iron gate that leaves the whole patio visible from the street while remaining physically closed. The house shows itself and stays shut; concealment is not the device here.",
      "note": "OVERRIDES a cascade fill from iberian-islamic ('the passage is bent or set off-axis specifically so the court is never visible from the street'), which is explicitly attributed to Moorish work in the family's own rule text and is the opposite of this node's own, best-known diagnostic tell: 'A patio fully visible from the street through an iron gate, and completely inaccessible' (diagnostic_tells point 1), and description, direct: 'Through it, from the street, a passerby can see the whole patio... and cannot enter. The house shows itself and stays shut.' Fixed here per WP-4.2 instructions rather than on the iberian-islamic family kit, which correctly scopes the bent-passage detail to Moorish work only -- the bug was this node inheriting the family's general framing without restating its own, opposite mechanism."
    },
    "setback_rule": {
      "group": "site",
      "binding": "specified",
      "status": "drafted",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rule": "Party-walled and built to the property line; there is no setback, and the block, not the individual house, is the unit of the street.",
      "note": "massing_affinities: townhouse-row 'canonical... these are party-walled urban houses in continuous blocks; the block, not the house, is the unit of the street.'"
    },
